From d258d824412971796de6b4f22a1a82d2c58faeb8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alexander Penev <alexander.p.penev@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 13:06:29 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] gpu: GLSL for the six HEP nodes, value and gradient

Without these a converted detector could not reach the GPU at all. The
emitter's switch did not know the kinds, and its plugin fallback could not
serve them: FRepNode::emit_glsl is handed the child expressions and a name
prefix but never the coordinates, and its own example writes the literal
names x, y, z. That is right for a node at the root and wrong under any
transform, where the point arrives as t3_x or as a Frame's rebuilt names.

glsl_hep.cpp mirrors hep_codegen.cpp for the value and hep_ad.cpp for the
dual, so there are now four descriptions of each solid - eval_t, IR, GLSL
value, GLSL dual - equal only because each was written against the last.
The tests are what make that a fact.

Both switches, not just the value one. A kind without a dual emitter sends
the WHOLE scene back to central-difference normals, which is the defect
measured on the CPU last commit (a Tube's |grad| reaching 1.092 at 10 m
because the step is absolute). Two helpers were added to the shader
preamble for it: d_div_s, a true divide so the dual matches the value path's
bits rather than multiplying by a reciprocal, and d_abs.

Every parameter goes through pval(), so one the policy made a runtime slot
is read from the shared buffer. That only began working for these kinds when
node_param_schema learned about them - before, slot_of() never matched and
every parameter silently baked.

Checked four ways, and this container can do all four:
  - all six kinds emit, no fallback and no CustomExpr;
  - the dual path survives, so normals stay analytic;
  - the shader compiles: 16208 SPIR-V words from 26109 chars of GLSL;
  - it RUNS on lavapipe and agrees with the CPU on a six-solid HEP scene -
    mean |difference| 0.00078 over 27648 channels, 3 of them past 0.05,
    which is the silhouette where a ray hits on one path and misses on the
    other.

Two defects the tests caught, both mine. d_abs was emitted BEFORE d_neg,
which it calls, and GLSL has no forward declarations - three compile errors
that only a real glslangValidator run finds. And the first version of the
SPIR-V check printed spv->size() as a word count: that function returns the
PATH of the .spv, so it reported 25 words for a 16208 word module. It now
stats the file.

